-1|0@news^1440353877#cud0fb3:Rain_Monkey$:Congratulations, and see you at the next circle dance, brother. My family had sex with the locals, just like yours. Overall, the argument still stands; we were here first, you get none, get out. Back on topic; immigration by the latest wave(s). The laws for immigration are being used both pro- and con-, and abused by both sides for their own agenda. I'm more interested in the underlying issues as we can probably both agree that the laws need some serious work. The only question is, how should they be re-formed. People often cite the issue from a fixed economy - if they are here, I get less. It goes like, "Those dirty Mexicans are stealing our jobs!" That idea ignores basic economics. More people = larger economy. If you really want to maintain the American middle class in today's globalized economy, you WANT immigration. In fact, you want a whole lot of immigration. US GDP growth is relatively stagnant and along with other Western economies, the long-term outlook is low due to the lack of growth. Immigration is kind of like free growth. If we can even encourage the brightest minds through programs, then we totally win, we get the people who have the greatest ideas. See Sergey Brin. Now let's look at illegal immigration. Let's try stopping it. Well, like the war on drugs, that ain't working out so good. Stopping it in one place just makes the flow go somewhere else. We could continue our current enforcement, which boils down to random occasional penalties, kind of like speeding. If we penalize enough people, then the general population knows they might get caught and reduce the overall rate. Unfortunately, when it comes to something as complex and life-changing as moving you and your family from one country to another, it just doesn't work. Then there's the simple human argument. I got to work the Muriel Boat Lift and the Haitian Boat lift. If you saw the people, and the conditions they were trying to escape, your opinions might change. If that was your family or your friends, your opinion might change. But overall for me is the simple question of human dignity. We claim to be a bastion of freedom and enterprise. How can we make that claim while closing borders? Who will we allow through - can we claim to know who will work hard and who will not? And how can we arbitrarily close them now, after over 400 years of claiming them to be open? The inscription on the Statue of Liberty says it, and far better than any 5-second sound bite. Give me your tired, your poor, Your huddled masses yearning to breathe free, The wretched refuse of your teeming shore. Send these, the homeless, tempest-tossed to me, I lift my lamp beside the golden door! THAT is America, and America's role to the world. THAT, along with education and infrastructure, is the magic formula to economic wealth.
